I am told that volume deliveries will not begin until the High Holidays.
Then of course there's the matter of system qualification, etc. I'm not
sure, but the low-end and midrange Alpha boxes may require some
modifications to their core logic chipsets, etc, to support ~1GHz
processors. Looking back over the past several years, a lag time of three
months or so between volume chip availability and initial system
availability is to be expected.

> Isn't Compaq interested in their image at all?  It might be
> nice to ship these chips right about now, while Intel is busy recalling
> their > 1GHz 8086 replacement chips.  I'm imagining some nice ad
opportunities.

If MHz was a be-all and end-all, having the fastest clock speed would indeed
be a Good Thing. Note that Intel is responsible for creating and fostering
the perception that MHz Matter Most; a misperception that just may come back
to haunt them when IA64 finally ships.

Methinks it's better to offer a processor that delivers mo' performance than
mo' MHz. HP would concur; PA-RISC may not be winning the MHz derby but the
architecture is no slouch when it comes to getting one's job done. The same
can be said for IBM'S POWER architecture.

And one might argue that Intel's latest processor gaffe is a direct result
of the firm's effort to maintain MHz bragging rights. Might it not have been
more prudent to delay availability of the chip until all the errata were
exorcised?

>
> Must the Q squander every advantage their engineers create?
>
> Besides, I want one of these chips in a VMS laptop.

The laptop would have to be at least three inches thick to accommodate the
heat sink for the chip in question...







