> become more and more hot, lots of graduate students from
> Mechanical Engineering or even Electrical Engineering are
> taking courses of java or database.Apparently, we can either
> find job in control or java, but taking java or database course
> won't help in control area job-hunting. Right?
> Maggie
>
Maggie,

I can't speak for the job market in control in general, but I can
certainly tell you what I look for in hiring for process control.  The
following items will probably be more appropriate for a small control
group than to a large one where people can afford to specialize more.

1)  Understanding of the processes being controlled -- metallurgy,
chemistry, properties, dynamics, etc...

2)  Practical work experience between school terms -- I look for
someone who actually went out and worked in a mill, refinery, chemical
plant, etc... during their summer breaks.  For grad student I look for
someone who did project work in the process industries.  I can not
stress highly enough how important I feel operations experience in the
process industries is.

3)  Control skills -- If you are in a PhD program in control I would
anticipate you would easily meet any requirements I might have for
theoretical control and process simulation.  I also look for the
ability to design control systems to fit real world cost, maintenance
and performance constraints.  In practice, I want people who can design
and tune control loops for best performance across a full range of
actual operating parameters.

4)  Optimizing or advanced control -- I look for someone who is
comfortable working with fuzzy logic rule sets, neural networks, and
model based controls as well as traditional feedback and feedforward
controls.

5)  Computer skills -- While I don't look for Java skills (yet), I do
look for database skills, strong Unix skills, shell scripting, AWK,
Perl, C, networking, Windows NT, etc.  Implementing control across a
variety of platforms and from the plant floor to the enterprise level
takes strong computer skills.  These skills are important even if you
have systems people doing most of the systems work for you.  It is also
important to be able to read ladder logic.

6)  Communication skills -- It is critically important to be able to
communicate clearly and effectively with the operators, technicians,
managers and co-workers.  The best control system design is useless if
you can't get it implemented, can't keep it maintained, or if the
operators can't work with it.

Hope my 2 cents here is of some help.  Please recognize that my
opinions offered above may not be very relevant for other industries or
companies.

Regards,

Kevin FitzGerrell
Process Control/Systems Admin
Fairbanks Gold Mining, Inc.
Fairbanks, Alaska


