> > someone who actually went out and worked in a mill, refinery,
chemical
> > plant, etc... during their summer breaks.  For grad student I look
for
> > someone who did project work in the process industries.  I can not
> > stress highly enough how important I feel operations experience in
the
> > process industries is.
> >
> > 3)  Control skills -- If you are in a PhD program in control I would
> > anticipate you would easily meet any requirements I might have for
> > theoretical control and process simulation.  I also look for the
> > ability to design control systems to fit real world cost,
maintenance
> > and performance constraints.  In practice, I want people who can
> design
> > and tune control loops for best performance across a full range of
> > actual operating parameters.
> >
> > 4)  Optimizing or advanced control -- I look for someone who is
> > comfortable working with fuzzy logic rule sets, neural networks, and
> > model based controls as well as traditional feedback and feedforward
> > controls.
> >
> > 5)  Computer skills -- While I don't look for Java skills (yet), I
do
> > look for database skills, strong Unix skills, shell scripting, AWK,
> > Perl, C, networking, Windows NT, etc.  Implementing control across a
> > variety of platforms and from the plant floor to the enterprise
level
> > takes strong computer skills.  These skills are important even if
you
> > have systems people doing most of the systems work for you.  It is
> also
> > important to be able to read ladder logic.
>
> I guess guys who know all of the listed skills will more likely
> to find a job outside of control. Positions in control area are
> not as many anyway.
>

Control is a good field for someone who wants to apply a knowledge from
a wide variety of disciplines.  I think this is a breadth vs. depth
issue.  While I use all of those skills, my level of knowledge varies.
For instance, I use Perl on a DCS system here because the reporting
features on that system are terrible, but I would not call myself a
Perl programmer.  I can read ladder logic well enough to verify that
"as implimented" matches "as designed".  I have to deal with loading,
interfacing and networking issues where they affect control, but I can
draw on experts as needed.  I work in a large gold mill where we have a
variety of chemical, comminution and separation processes, and my
understanding of those processes is critical to my ability to design
good control for them.

You are certaily correct that there are fewer positions in control
engineering than in many other engineering fields.  I find the work
very rewarding though.

> >
> > 6)  Communication skills -- It is critically important to be able to
> > communicate clearly and effectively with the operators, technicians,
> > managers and co-workers.  The best control system design is useless
if
> > you can't get it implemented, can't keep it maintained, or if the
> > operators can't work with it.
> >
> >
>
> I have the feeling that control engineers have been asked
> too much while do not get recognized (if the design, cost,
> performance and everything else go fine). That will push
> people with relative talent away from this area, which
> who knows is good or not.

I seem to recall from recent salary surveys that control engineering
pays less than the average engineering job.  I would guess the level of
recognition depends more on the individual company however.  I believe
that a control engineer needs a wider knowledge base than many other
engineering disciplines, however if I was a civil engineer I might feel
the same way about civil engineering. :)  I have seen many companies
moving away from in house control engineering to outside engineering
firms.  This trend should attract those engineers interested in a more
closely defined area of specialization.

