Steve,

The DECpc XL 590s you have may all have an older main logic board that
is implemented to an early early PCI hardware specification, most
likely PCI 1.0.  Check the DEC part number inscribed on the main logic
board.  The part number is not the serial number stickered on the main
logic board.  If the part number is not 54-23606 or the BIOS is 1.21,
you have an old board.  The old board also has a serious BIOS
limitation for IDE hard drives.

The Celebris XL uses the 54-23606 board, and maybe some of the DECpc
XLs do, too.  I have had not trouble with Matrox graphics cards (even
Millenium II) and I think I threw an Adaptec 2940 into a Celebris XL
once, too.

PCI sound cards and some PCI network cards may be problematic, even in
the 54-23606 board.  Both DECpc XL and Celebris XL use the earliest
Intel NX "Neptune" chipset, which may impose a limitation.  And
computer card manufacturers in their anxiety to get the highest
performing PCI cards to market in the shortest time often sacrifice
backward compatibility with PCI 1.0 (and even PCI 2.0).

I have a few new 54-23606 boards left in stock here.  Also faster
Intel processors with DEC part numbers and heat sinks on them, and the
more modern Celebris XL front plastic panel.  If you're interested in
upgrading, get in touch.  Our prices are pretty reasonable... Ben 

