Organization: A private user of MCSnet
NNTP-Posting-Date: Sat, 26 Aug 2000 12:33:47 MST
Newsgroups: comp.sys.dec

< I have an AlphaStation 200 4/166, and I got a working 266MHz CPU
< out of a scrapped AlphaServer 200 4/266. Is ist possible to get the
< faster CPU working with 266MHz in the AlphaStation?

Not that I've ever seen.  It is possible to get the system to run at
233 MHz (essentially it becomes an AS200-4/233 system).  My AS200
was a 4/166, I bought a 275MHz CPU from a Cabriolet board and upgraded
the system to 233 without any problems.  This is _not_ an overclock,
it was my rendition of a supported upgrade path.  VMS recognizes it
as a 4/233 system, and it is noticeably quicker, though benchmark
tests did not reveal an improvement comparable to the clock speed
increase.

You will need an RF Monolithics HC1360 oscillator (466.67MHz) to 
replace the HC1282 (333MHz) unit in your system; these are no longer
sold anywhere I could find, but I was able to get a few sales samples
(all used in upgrades, sorry) which worked fine.

You also need to move a jumper (J30 on the mainboard).  This is not 
documented in the later manuals, but my earlier one did.  Per that
you need to install a  jumper for pins 1 & 2 for 233MHz operation,
leave it off for 166MHz.  Pin 3 and 4 are not to be jumpered either
way.

Do any of this at your own risk; it worked for me, but if you fry your
system, its your own call.

Rich Jordan
rjordan@mcs.net




