In article <399C7038.FACCB2CC@fnal.gov>, Maarten Litmaath <litmaath@fnal.gov> writes:
|> has anyone got a reasonable performance out of a Gb Ethernet card
|> connected to a recent Alpha box running OSF1/DUNIX V4.0F or higher?

Probably it depends somewhat on which "recent Alpha box" you
refer to, but (for example) one of my colleagues has measured
ca. 967 Mbits/sec. process-to-process TCP throughput using
a pair of XP1000 (667MHz) systems, Tru64 V4.0F.  (That 967
Mbits/sec is TCP data; if you count TCP+IP+Ethernet headers,
it's pretty close to full line speed.)  Depending on the
precise details of the test, both hosts had some CPU time
left over.

This requires the use of 9000-byte packets (supported by
the DEGPA-SA card we sell, but apparently not by all switch
vendors) and some attention to socket buffer sizes.

I know that the same hardware can get similar results running
with a recent version of Linux, but this requires using a
particular version of the driver for the Gbit interface.

Also, our recent SPECWeb99 results use the DEGPA-SA
Gbit Ethernet card; see
   http://www.spec.org/osg/web99/results/res2000q2/web99-20000403-00023.html
for an example.  This page shows what the Tru64 tuning parameters
were set to, in order to get this kind of results.  (I don't know
how to translate SPECweb99 results into network throughput, though.)

The basic answer is that "your performance will vary", based on
	hardware
	application
	tuning parameters
but it's certainly not impossible to get good results.

-Jeff


