The RA81 is a 456MB winchester disk. I do not quite understand
the comments regarding its size. I mean the RA80 is exactly the
same size hand weight and stored only 121 MB. The RA82 was
the last member in this family and had a 622MB capacity.
All capacities are formatted, useful capacities.
I think the RA81 draws about 12 A of current (220V) when it spins up.
In steady state it took about 800 Watts or so.
Be sure to have the terminator for the power sequencing circuit in place
otherwise the unit won't start up. Open the drawer (either by pressing a
lever in the center of the lower front air inlet or two nuts at the sides,
depends
on whether you have an early model or not) and check whether the belt is
tightened. Inside, on the right towards the front there's a piece of steel
wire
attached to a spring mechanism to tighten the belt.
On top of the HDA is a lever to unlock the heads. If the unit was shipped
with
the handle in the unlock position, don't bother : all you've got is an
oversized
paperweight.
The units uses a Digital proprietary interface. It connects to:
- UDA50    Unibus    VAX 11/7xx, PDP11/x4
- KDA50    Q-bus
- KDB50   BI bus
- KDM70  XMI bus (VAX 6000/7000)
- HSC controllers

The RA81 was fairly reliable for such an ancient unit. The last ones were
retired
three years ago because DEC offered us a 9GB fast/wide SCSI drive for each
functional RA8x or RA60 drive.
The units are obsolete and belong in a museum. Even for hobbyists these
beasts are far too expensive to operate.
But I've got to admit that several SA482's seemed to impress visitors a lot
more
than a couple of BA356 cabinets...

Hans Vlems



