In comp.os.vms David A Froble <davef@tsoft-inc.com> wrote:
> I've got a used DECserver 700, no documentation.  I've looked on both COmpaq and
> Cabletron sites for such, and no luck.  I am rather poor at finding stuff on web
> pages, so the fault's probably mine.

  No, DEC/Compaq's web pages are incredibly badly organized.

  The software manuals are at:
http://www.networks.digital.com/dr/dnas/manuals/

  There's more info at:
http://www.networks.digital.com/dr/hubs/servers/

  But the DS700 manual isn't there, for some unknown reason. There's a copy
at:
http://www.networks.digital.com/dr/servers/manuals/dsrvw-mg.pdf

> When powered up, connected with a 10baseT connection, I get no response on port
> 1, terminal set to 9600 baud.  The green light by the 10baseT connection blinks,
> indicating (I'd think) network activity.  The light by the power cord is off. 
> The led blinks, what I'd call an 'A' without the cross bar, or maybe an 'n', or
> maybe it's upside down, and it's a 'U'.

  The green LED near the network port doesn't show anything useful if the LED
by the power cord is off or blinking. The fact that the second LED is blinking
says that the server is unhappy.

  The error you have is described in the last manual I listed above, on page
73 of the PDF (manual page 7-9). It says "No SIMs or wrong type SIMs installed"
so it's likely somebody mugged your DS700 for its memory. It's a generic 72-
pin SIMM - as I recall, it's FPM, parity (x36), gold pins, 80ns or faster.

