in article 8msqpm$p0n$1@news6.svr.pol.co.uk, john muir at
john@brobdignag.freeserve.co.uk on 8/9/00 7:43 PM wrote something like:

> I have a dec 3000/300lx with a monitor but no keyboard.
> the back shows a keyb/pointer icon over a 25 pin male 'D' plug.
> Anyone know hardware specifics such as what pin should connect to what,
> and if its possible to modify a generic pc keyboard to do the job.
> i have already tried to match the various voltages etc to a pc
> keyboard/mouse output
> but can make any sense of it. any help will be greatly appreciated.
> cheers    -jemida gabber.    [farmerATcyberspaceDOTorg]

    Well the part you need is a DEC cable that has the DB-15 on one end and
an RJ-11 keyboard connector and a PS-2 style mouse port. They are however
*not* PC compatible control signals. You will need a DEC LK-20x or LK-40x
keyboard and a DEC VSXXX-xA mouse. I have about five spare LK-20x or 40x
keyboards laying around in my closet, I don't have a spare mouse however.
    This information was posted from Hoff, which may prove useful. I don't
have the pinouts for the DB-15 and KB handy but I can dig them up if you
need them.



:Can anyone please provide pinout for a VSxxx-aa type of mouse?

  Pinout: 1: signal GND; 2:TXD; 3:RXD; 4:-12V; 5:+5V; 6: not used;
  7: device present -- shorted to pin 1; shell: protective ground.

  End view of mouse cable connector:

            +
          5 6 7
          3   4
          +1 2+

  Where + indicates a connector key.

  Signalling is RS232-compatible 4800 baud 9 bit bytes (8 data, 1 bit
  for parity (odd)) with mark <-6V and space >+4.6V on transmit, and
  mark -15 to 0.8V and space 2.8 to +15V on receive, with minimum DC
  load of 3000 ohms to ground...  Reports are via three-byte `tuples'.
  Here are the meanings of the bits in the tuples:

  Bits 7 to 0:                Byte:
  1 0  0  SX SY L  M  R       0
  0 X6 X5 X4 X3 X2 X1 X0      1
  0 Y6 Y5 Y4 Y3 Y2 Y1 Y0      2

  Bit 7 is the frame synch
  Sx, SY: sign bit: positive (1) or negative (0).
  L,M,R: mouse button state: down (1) or up (0).
  Xn and Yn are X & Y displacements, with n0 as LSB.
  If X and/or Y overflows, the maximum is reported.

:Can this thing be adapted to pc plug?

  The typical VSXXX-nn `hockey puck' mouse is not normally considered
  compatible with the typical PC mouse...

  Most any problem of this class is surmountable (or `surface-mountable'
  :-) with enough solder and the appropriate ICs and discrete parts...
  But not nearly as easily nor as cheaply as one can acquire a PC mouse
  or a PC trackball... 




