In article <Iumk5.18817$NH2.153000@typhoon.ne.mediaone.net>, "Terry C. Shannon" <terryshannon@mediaone.net> writes:
:> In comp.os.vms Ramon L. Tate <taterskins@patriot.net> wrote:
..
:> Once our halon system was set off accidentaly when an air-conditioning
:> repair man was working on one of the three AC systems in the computer
:> room.  A freon tank he was using became unplugged, and started venting
:> freon into the room.  Freon gas is detected by the computer room's fire
:> systems, which set off the halon system.
:
:Both Freon and Halon (1301 and 1411) are halogenated hydrocarbons, hence
:they are somewhat related. But I've never heard of a computer room fire
:protection system that would trip as a result of sensing Halon (or Freon) in
:the atmosphere. Normally it's heat or incipient products of combustion
:that'll dump a Halon system.

  Correct, I'd not expect the gas dump itself to trigger the sensors.

  Blowing up a cloud of dust can trip sensors.  (Some are notoriously
  sensitive to concrete dust, for instance.)  Usually, these sensors
  are grouped into zones and only one will get clogged at a time (and 
  thus only triggers a warning, rather than a system dump).  But stir 
  up enough dust and you could quite conceivably fool enough adjacent 
  zones in the fire protection system and thus trigger a system dump.

  Also, every so often, somebody working on HVAC manages to ignite nearby
  materials and trigger a fire -- as AC usually involves fans, and gasses
  from any fire could very easily trigger multiple sensors.  Worse, 
  occasionally somebody working on an AC system manages to ignite the 
  freon, and you really do not want to be around for the combustion 
  byproducts of that particular chemical reaction.  (Unlike more "normal" 
  fires, which can produce some lesser but still very nasty byproducts, 
  such as hydrogen cyanide gas.)

:> ...The cost to recharge our halon tanks was about 20K
:> USD each.  But we really don't have to worry about that anymore.  About 3
:> years ago, they renovated the computer room, and turned off the halon
:> system during the construction.  They've never bothered to turn the halon
:> system back on.  They probably want to save money.  So now there are about
:> 6 fire extinguishers in the computer room.  It's the responsibility of the
:> computer room workers to put any fires out themselves with the fire
:> extinguishers.

  I would hope there was some training, as correct use of a fire
  extinguisher isn't nearly as easy as it might seem, and there are
  also cases where the goal of the occupants should be escape and
  not fire suppression -- obviously most folks are not trained in 
  fire suppression.  (Even those that are trained in fire suppression
  are typically trained to exit the area unless they have the proper 
  personal protective equipment, and particularly the proper respiratory 
  protective equipment.  A "computer" is not worth "dead", nor is it
  worth the exposure to any of the various lung-damaging chemicals 
  regularly produced by the combustion of modern materials.)
  
:A couple of reasons the Halon might have been turned off. Halon used to be
:cheap; maybe a buck a pound. During the late 70's - early 80's the price cli
:mbed to ~$25 per pound. Seems that Halon does bad things to the ozone layer,
:just like Freon and other halogens. I believe the US is a signatory to some
:UN treaty banning, or sharply diminishing, the use of the stuff...

  Correct.  There is apparently one new/potential substitute for use 
  in quenching the free radical reaction (no, that's neither a fringe 
  group nor a rock band :-) necessary as part of the chemical reaction
  within fire, and thus useful within flooding fire suppression systems.

:Based on the not-inconsiderable expense of Halon, system tests involving the
:fire suppression agent itself are rare these days. Typically the system is
:charged with nitrogen for the qualification test, then recharged with Halon
:when it's placed into service.

  More than a few sites have discontinued use of these extinguishing
  compounds -- a few sites have replaced these systems with total-flood
  (oxygen displacement) nitrogen or carbon dioxide systems, which will 
  suffocate the fire but will also have, um, interesting properties for 
  anything that tries to breath within the room.  Well, anything that
  is not wearing self-contained breathing apparatus (SCBA) or other 
  similar source of breathing air.

  Hoff
  (state-certified hose-dragger, among other sidelines)

