> 'Wildfire' is a trademark of 'Wildfire Communications'.
> It should _not_ be used for the AlphaServer GS80/160/320.
> 
> > Now, the "SAN Application note" tells me,
> > OpenVMS requires multipath failover mode and SCSI-3
> > Tru64 requires transparent failover mode and SCSI-2.
> 
> It is Tru64 Unix version 4f/g that have this limits.
> Can you use version 5.0A? (I beleive V5.1 isn't there, yet)
> 
> > How should i configure the HSG80�s to use the ESA
> > from both Systems????
> 
> When I have *played* with this, OpenVMS did work with the HSG80s
> being in transparent failover mode, but not with SCSI-2!
> (It configured the CCL as a disk and ignored all other units.)
> 
> I don't know if you can run Tru64 Unix V4 with the HSG80s
> being in transparent failover mode and SCSI-3.
> 
> I am afraid you cannot run OpenVMS + Tru64 Unix V4 on the same
> HSG80 in a 'supported' configuration.

probably not in a 'supported' config, but it should work in
multipath failover mode.  The problem with Tru64 V4.X and multipath
failover arises when you have more than one FibreChannel HBA (Host Bus
Adapter) in the Tru64 machine, in which case the system will see
a single HSG disk (let's say "D1") via each HBA, and because V4 doesn't
normally (see below) understand multiple paths to the same device,
but rather uses the Bus/Target/Lun combination to uniquely identify
a disk, it will conclude that it sees a separate disk for each HBA.

As I was writing the above it occurred to me that there is a product (from
Compaq, I think), called SecurePath which, if I remember correctly, allows
Tru64 V4 systems to take advantage of multipath functionality... so you
might be able to run your VMS and Tru64 systems from the same HSG pair
in a supported config after all.

Rob Urban



