The free radicals (Cl, also ClO, CLO2) are quite reactive; they will
eventually recombine, but it may take years.  But the principal source
for these radicals is UV radiation: UV splits the free radicals off
chloroflurocarbons and other stratospheric chemicals. since the ozone
layer blocks most of the UV wavlength at the right wavelength, the Cl
free-radicals only

See http://www.faqs.org/faqs/ozone-depletion/stratcl/


>...and yes I *AM* talking about bleaches and disinfectants - names don't
>"magically" make chemical agents any less formidable. 


Again, see the FAQ or other resources.  Most atmospheric chlorine
compounds, whether organic or inorganic, are usualy oxized in the
troposhere and then rained out before they rise high enough for UV to
photolyze them.

So. In order for a Cl compund to create stratospheric chlorine
radicals, it has to be inert enough to get through the troposphere
without being broken down (eventually into HCl) then rained
out. Effective bleaches and disinfectants are, naturally,
fairly reactive. 


