In article <39969596.91AF66D1@gmx.de>, Dirk Boeing <dirk.boeing@gmx.de> writes:
> Hi!
> 
> Last week I had the chance to get a DEC 3000/600, so I took it home as
> fast as I could. The actual operation system is VMS, but I would like to
> install Linux or BSD on it. So I inserted a bootable CD-ROM in the drive
> and started the computer, thinking of installing the operation system
> the same way as on Intel computers, but nothing happened. The machine
> ignores the bootable CD and starts to boot VMS. As I have absolutely no
> experience with alpha systems and even no manuals of the computer
> system, I don't know what to do now. 

No offense intended, but in general assuming that an Alpha behaves
like an Intel machine is not a useful process.

> Is there a special command I must give to boot from the CD-ROM? There
> seems to be no possibility to interrupt the boot-process and to tell the
> computer to boot from another device. When I press that kind of
> reset-button on the backside of the computer or give the shutdown
> command in VMS, I get a >>> prompt but I can't access the CD-ROM drive
> from there.

What commands did you try to make you feel you cannot access
the CD-ROM drive ?

		>>> SHOW DEVICE

should list hardware devices, including the CDROM drive.  The
factory default puts the CDROM at DKA400, but someone might
have changed the hardware.

So if it seems to be DKA400, the boot command would be:

		>>> BOOT DKA400

There are variants to combat/deal-with strange settings
on the disk you are booting, but if your BSD disk is like
the VMS installation disks, it is arranged to be particularly
non-fussy about those.

=====

Of course you should make sure the DEC 3000/600 is listed
as compatible with the CD-ROM and operating system you are
booting.



