"Terry C. Shannon" wrote:
[snip]
> Both Freon and Halon (1301 and 1411) are halogenated hydrocarbons, 

Well, not to nit-pick, but "Freon" is a DuPont tradename for their
halogentaed hydrocarbon refrigerant products: Freon-12 (R-12,
dichlorodiflouromethane), Freon-22 (R-22, monchlorodiflouromethane) and
others. Other trade names of other producers are such as "Cotron",
"Isotron", etc.

Not sure who came up with "Halon".

Prior to all the hoo-hah about ozone depletion, it was common practice
to vent the entire charge of small refrigeration systems to the
atmosphere. Such practice is now illegal, as it is believed that such
compounds find their way into the upper atmosphere where they are (so
the studies say) broken down by sunlight releasing free chlorine which
attacks O3 (ozone) and reduces it to O2.

I did Refrigeration & HVAC for a living prior to EDP. I once found
myself in a basement faced with a water-cooled R-12 system that someone
had charged with R-22. Needless to say, the compressor was scrambled. We
blew the charge getting ready to swap the pump when my partner tried to
light up a cigaraette and almost killed himself: halo-carbon
refrigerants produce noxious and poisonous gasses when consumed by fire.

If free chlorine is truly the ozone threat, then why not ban the use of
chlorine in swimming pools (tons of chlorine-bearing compunds in solid
form are used every year), and the use of sodium hypochlorite solution
(chlorine bleach) for laundry and sanitation (100s of thousands, if not
millions of gallons daily), not to mention the tons and tons of chlorine
used in drinking water supplies every month across the country and
around the world?

