A couple of reasons the Halon might have been turned off. Halon used to be
cheap; maybe a buck a pound. During the late 70's - early 80's the price cli
mbed to ~$25 per pound. Seems that Halon does bad things to the ozone layer,
just like Freon and other halogens. I believe the US is a signatory to some
UN treaty banning, or sharply diminishing, the use of the stuff (although
the threat imposed by millions of old refrigerators and junk cars with A/C
would seem to be many orders of magnitude higher than the threat of a few
thousand fire protection systems that only dump when they have to do so).

Incidentally, my last encounter with a Halon system was in Syracuse, NY in
the 1970's. I helped design the system, so I got to go along on the test.
When the Halon tank dumped, it didn't leave any residue (Halon generally
doesn't do that), but it did make one hell of a lot of noise while
instantaneously rearranging all the ceiling tiles.

Based on the not-inconsiderable expense of Halon, system tests involving the
fire suppression agent itself are rare these days. Typically the system is
charged with nitrogen for the qualification test, then recharged with Halon
when it's placed into service.

