I think the halocarbons are rather inert, and migrate fairly easily
to the upper atmosphere.  There they are broken down into a form that
may have something to do with ozone depletion that may be happening, and
may be partly caused by something human folks are doing.

Free chlorine is extremely reactive, and won't hang around in that
form long enough to get to the upper atmosphere and try to mess with
the ozone.  It's much more likely to corrode your car or kill a
nasty germ than to get to the high-altitude o-zone.

