> I think the Freon (and Halon, too) acts as an ion trap, sucking up the
ions
> that are being produced by that bit of radioactive material in the fire
> detector and activating it just like fire combustion products do. At least
> that's my understanding of how the ionization-type detectors and Halon
> work.

Could be, physics is not one of my stronger suits. I've seen (heard,
actually) ionization-detectors go off in the midst of particularly violent
thunderstorms, so anything's possible. As for extinguishing fires, Halon
works as a chain-breaking agent that whacks the free radicals that are
produced in the combustion process (e.g. it doesn't smother, cool, or remove
the combustible product).

The downside is that while Halon itself is safe, some of its products of
decomposition are not. When heated, Halon decomposes into such goodies as
phosgene. We learned this the hard way early in the century when carbon
tetrachloride (aka cleaning fluid, and a poison in its own right) fire
extinguishers were popular. After a few people were extinguished in the
process of extinguishing fires, carbon tet (Pyrene) fire extinguishers were
banned.

Some of these things are still, umm, hanging around. If you ever see a
six-inch-diameter glass globe filled with a pinkish fluid hanging from the
ceiling of an old building (there are metal variants of this thing as well),
get rid of the thing. It's a "Red Comet" carbon tet fire extinguisher.

