OK, this is an area where I know something...

The chemical energy to heat energy transfer efficiency that Terry speaks of
could well be 80%.  You know some heat goes up the smokestack.  The 30% figure
that others have mentioned is the heat energy to mechanical energy conversion
of the "heat engine" -- Carnot or turbine or Watt.  Thermodynamics limits the
heat to mechanical efficiency depending on the ratio of temperature difference
to the absolute temperature of the colder (environment), which for reasonable
combustion and steam temperatures and a hot summer day's cooling tower is going
to be on the order of 30%.

Nuclear plants always have two circulation systems:  primary coolant (typically
air, water, or melted sodium metal) that is in direct contact with the fuel
assemblies and is exposed to neutron bombardment, therefore becoming
contaminated with radioactive materials, and the secondary coolant, typically
water that is turned to steam to run the turbines to drive the generator.
There is always some efficiency lost in the transfer of heat between the
primary coolant and the secondary coolant.

                                RDP


