Totally off-topic for these newsgroups, but: you've confused two very
different things: "free" (unoxidized) chlorine, and chlorine radicals.

Cloroflurocarbons, once relased into the atmosphere, can migrate
through the atmosphere into the stratosphere, where ionizing radiation
can strip off the chlorine atom, prodcing free chlorine radicals (Cl,
_not_ Cl2). The free chlorine radicals are very reactive, and they
will eventually recombine with other chemicals. The problem is that
when free chlorine radicals react wtih ozone (O3), they break
down the O3 into O2 and re-release the free chlorine radical.

Cl2 from the sources you mention (aqueous solution) doesn't make it
into the stratosphere; it tends ot react with other chemicals
first. (Youre' talking about bleaches and antibacterial agentts, sheesh!)

This really isn't much beyond high-school chemistry.



