Chlorine has been a basis of industrial products for more than a century. 
If I remember correctly, natural sources of chlorine (for instance from
volcanic eruptions) produce more of it by an order of magnitude or two than
that released through human intervention, and sources such as volcanoes push
it straight past the troposphere in some cases. Nonetheless, the ozone layer
appears to have been pretty stable until CFCs reached it and started their
handiwork, so the cause-and-effect relationship, and the exoneration of
"normal" chlorine, seems clear.

To get nearer to the original subject, I agree with Charlie Matco's reasoning
that it would have been reasonable - given the general exceptions granted to
developing countries - to include an exception for Halon in its use as a fire
extinguisher/retardent. It's quite superior to CO2 or Argon for this use, and
the amount leaked can't be significant compared to all sources for CFCs in the
environment.

	Jan



