In article <8n21ie$7lo$1@mailint03.im.hou.compaq.com>, hoffman@xdelta.zko.dec.nospam (Hoff Hoffman) writes:
>  More than a few sites have discontinued use of these extinguishing
>  compounds -- a few sites have replaced these systems with total-flood
>  (oxygen displacement) nitrogen or carbon dioxide systems, which will 
>  suffocate the fire but will also have, um, interesting properties for 
>  anything that tries to breath within the room.  Well, anything that
>  is not wearing self-contained breathing apparatus (SCBA) or other 
>  similar source of breathing air.

At the telecommunications company I worked at, the computer rooms
had fairly normal (for the time) gas discharge extinquishers.  I
don't know if they ever used Halon or Freon, I think it was always
CO2.

The wire room (the place where all of the communications lines
came together for interconnection) was thought to need special
protection, so it was in an enclosed space within the enclosed
computer area, with extra security.  There was also a special
level of alarms set for that room, and every employee that
might ever come near the room was told to leave the room
immediately if the alarm ever went off, because it would be
completely floodded with CO2 very rapidly.  The room several
air masks available, and the people who worked in the area
regularly were trained on how to use them if they somehow didn't
get out of the room quickly enough.  I'm happy to say that in
the time I was there the equipment was never used (except, I
assume, for testing and training).

It seems extream, but given that the operation of the company
depended upon this room remaining intact, the measures used
seem reasonable.  And they did train all of the people affected.


