Peter LANGSTOEGER (eplan@kapsch.net) wrote:
: I want to add an external SCSI Disk (a CDROM). There is a big connector
: on the back which I call a SCSI-3 (68pin ; and don't rant ;-).

What you want to do is not supported: you are not supposed to use both
the internal, and external chains of KZPBA-CA. (Either one, by itself,
is fine).

: wonder. I never connected an external terminator, but internal disks work.
: Why ? Is there an console mode parameter ? Or is there an intelligent
: terminator inside ?

There is an on-board terminator, which can terminate either the complete
bus, or just the upper eight bits. There is a pkc_term console variable
which was apparently intended to control this; however, the au-series
SRM manual labels it as "unused". You *may* be able to control the termination
settings by switching to the ARC console, and invoking the on-board
configuration utility during the boot, but I wasn't able to make this
work.

: However connecting a SCSI-1 to SCSI-3 Cable, the RRD42 and a terminator
: externally does hang the SCSI Bus (VMS freezes or console mode tells that
: disk DKA100 does not poll).

You *may* be able to get it to work by replacing the internal ribbon cable
with a shorter one, and using the shortest possible cables for the external
chain. The ribbon cable which shipped with au-series uses up all the chain
length allowed for UltraSCSI ...

You'll probably have better luck by borrowing an internal SCSI CD-ROM.

Cheers,

/Serge.P

