Art Rice <arice.NOSPAM@ue.itug.org> writes:
>"Jack Peacock" <peacock@simconv.com> wrote:
>
>>"Jonathan Stone" wrote:

>>> Cloroflurocarbons, once relased into the atmosphere, can migrate
>>> through the atmosphere into the stratosphere, where ionizing radiation
>>> can strip off the chlorine atom, prodcing free chlorine radicals (Cl,
>>> _not_ Cl2). The free chlorine radicals are very reactive, and they
>>> will eventually recombine with other chemicals. The problem is that
>>> when free chlorine radicals react wtih ozone (O3), they break
>>> down the O3 into O2 and re-release the free chlorine radical.

>>Which begs the question, if the end result is O2 then why doesnt the
>>same ionizing radiation convert the O2 to O3?  Isn't that where the
>>ozone comes from in the first place?  

http://www.faqs.org/faqs/ozone-depletion/

CFCs decay via uv and the free Cl forms other molecules that decompose
at a low rate which is none-the-less enough to do damage to the ozone
layer.

Because the stratosphere is dry those other molecules do not wash out
and so are still around when nighttime comes to the poles, which is when
the most dramatic and best understood damage to the ozone is done.

O3 is formed by UV but the rate of formation is much below that of
CFC induced destruction.

>It was my understanding that the major contributor in the production
>of ozone is Lightning.

Tropospheric (the layer next to the earth) ozone under no-human conditions.

Robert Morphis



