Hi Wolfgang,

I had a VS3100/76SPX do a similar thing.  I powered it off when
I went on holiday for a week.  Afterward, it froze during exercise
of the graphics subsystem, and would not boot.

If the 3100/76 and the 3200 are similar enough (I never used a 3200),
disconnect the monitor, keyboard and mouse.  Attach a VT300/400 etc.
to the console I/O port.  I'm guessing it's a 6-wire MMJ connector, but
might
be a DB25 or something else.  You may have to try a few different ports
unless someone else provides a definitive answer.  Perhaps there is a
switch on the back of the hardware to toggle the console/printer port.

Set the terminal for 9600 8N1 (once again, that's a best guess).
Power on the machine and, hopefully, you'll shortly see the diagnostic
countdown and the console prompt.

One caution:  once the machine is booted in this mode, you may have
difficulty with the VMS license.  The machine now thinks of itself as a
"server" instead of a "workstation".  Your previous license will be good
for only one user (from any location) and one additional login from the
console port, provided the other user was logged in first.

-Mike


