Steam is nothing.

I was involved in deploying and testing a cluster of servers for one
of the national newspapers in the UK, it held all their pictures for
example. We completed the system and applications testing on
the thursday with the initial go live being monday. As I left I commented
to the datacenter manager that their machine room felt pretty hot.

On the monday we got a call telling us not to come in and letting us
know that the system wasn't going live.

Worried we phoned back and after a few abortive calls got the low down.
Seems that the aircon unit was faulty and so the customer had shut
the systems down on friday and two aircon engineers had arrived on saturday,
dissassembled the whole system including a high pressure water feed
above the systems in the roof void and then re-assembled the whole thing.

Or so they thought, they forgot to tighten up one of the joints in the water
feed and when they turned the water back on at the valve which was a
few minutes walk from the machine room a few thousand gallons of slightly
gritty (calcium) water was dumped over the 6 machines in the cluster.

The engineers turned the water feed back off and one of them did a runner
leaving his colleague to carry the can.

The machines were soaked, water in the disk trays, water behind the LED
covers on the diag units and fine calcium grit over everything.

We dried the machines out and cleaned them which took about a week and
all 6 systems re-started without a single problem. Sadly they were
subsequently
junked because they could not be warrantied having been drowned and they
were replaced.

3 months later the people in the offices below started to complain about
damp and an inspection of the sub-floor revealed that there was still
3 inches of water under all the systems. Still that must have kept the
relative humidity up.

