Once upon a time when I worked as a junior student systems programmer
for
Dundee College of Technology I remember dialing in to the DEC-20
remotely (300 baud) and continually getting %DECSYSTEM-20 NOT RUNNING
followed by [DECSystem-20 Continued] a few seconds later. The first
error indicated some kind of serious problem and usually meant the
system had crashed. If it was followed by the continued message then
TOPS-20 had managed to take corrective action to work around
the problem. I had a quick look at the error logs and found parity
errors, front end reloads, powerfail restarts, Massbus errors,
I/O subsystem restarts etc. There was the occasional complete
reboot but on most occasions the system was able to recover. Obviously
something was far wrong.

It was a Sunday night and on the Monday the DEC engineer (Ed Merle (sp?)
later to become a senior figure in DEC FS, turned up looked at the
temp/humidity charts and lifted a floor tile . Almost up to tile level
was about 6 inches of water. Every single cable was entirely submerged
and water was dripping down the CPU and memory blown by fans. Several
single phase outlets had blown but amazingly the 3-phase supply was
intact and a very soggy DEC-20 was finally rapidly brought down by
one very distressed engineer (realising he was sitting on a lake
with live power all around) hitting the nearest emergency stop
button.
--
Alan Greig


