Yep. No Such Agency's downlink in Alice Springs, Australia being one such
venue. I recall some Convex folks being way torqued off at DEC for some VAX
9000 vs C-Series benchmarks... seems that the 9000 had a screw somewhere
inside it which enabled Field Service to crank up the clock speed. IIRC the
9000 had a 15ns cycle time; the system used for the benchmark bake-off was
cranked to 9ns.

I believe that at least one water-cooled Aquarius prototype was built, but
DEC elected to say no to plumbers and instead went with air cooling. This
cut the VUPpage down to ~21 from ~30 in the water-cooled model.

The 9000 was not one of DEC's flaming successes. Total sales of 454 systems.
And many problems with the box... which came as no surprise given DEC's
decision to rely on unproven high density signal carrier (HDSC) technology.
A Risky VAX Scheme, it was!





