On 8 Aug 2000 13:38:47 PDT, abuse@flying-disk.com (Alan Frisbie)
wrote:

>I am having an odd problem with my VAX 4000-200 (KA660).
>When I power it up, I get the usual language prompt
>followed by the diagnostic count-down: 95..94..93..92...
>
>When it gets to 65.. (memory byte test), it simply stays
>there "forever" (I haven't actually waited more than a
>couple of hours, however).   The system has two MS650-BA
>boards.   If I remove the ribbon cable from one or the
>other of the boards, the symptom stays the same.   If
>I disconnect both of them, I get a fatal error much
>earlier in the countdown (about 91 or so).
>
>How long should test 65 take with two memory boards (32 MB total)?
>
>Since test 66 (memory data) was previously passed, I
>suspect that the memory is basically "working".   I find
>it very odd that a diagnostic would just hang without an
>error message, especially when the basic test passed OK.
>
>Does anyone have any insight that might help me?
>
>Thanks,
>Alan

Alan:

Not that I can *really* help you but the failure to produce the next
pass code i.e., 64 is the error code. VAXes are very cryptic when it
comes to these sorts of things. Even in the 4000 era EPROMs and PROMs
were pretty expensive so most the of the console code had to fit in a
very small space with no room for descriptive error messages.

As an ex hardware tech at DEC I found that these things usually only
made sense when I tried to look at from the same viewpoint as the guy
who designed it. Definitely not from the same viewpoint of the guy
trying to fix it.

There are/were listing of what each code represented in terms which
bit of hardware was tested and passed, but I couldn't tell you where
to find them.


Jman...

