What about the potential for disaster?

At the university where I work, the main computer server room is on the
first floor.  On the second floor, there are biology labs.  In between the
servers, and the pipes supplying water to the labs on the second floor is
a lovely tile ceiling.  The pipes have leaked in the past.  What do we do?
We put plastic sheeting on top of our equipment of course (only when the
pipes are leaking, though).  There is also an incinerator on the second
floor.  Which has caused at least one fire so far.  How was the fire put
out by the fire department?  With water of course.

The university is in the city of New Orleans.  In case you don't know, New
Orleans has the third highest average rainfall rate in the US.  What
happens when it rains hard enough at the university?  Water leaks into the
building, running to the lowest point, the one foot of space in the
sub-flooring of the computer room.  (To be fair, they have recently
installed a large drain outside the door where the water leaks into the
building).

Once our halon system was set off accidentaly when an air-conditioning
repair man was working on one of the three AC systems in the computer
room.  A freon tank he was using became unplugged, and started venting
freon into the room.  Freon gas is detected by the computer room's fire
systems, which set off the halon system.  But only one of the four halon
tanks had any halon left in them.  The halon had leaked out of the other
tanks over time.  They started testing the halon tanks after that.
When the halon system "blew" it destroyed some ceiling tiles, and left a
fine powder over everything.  Guess who had to vacuum the entire room and
all of it's contents?  The cost to recharge our halon tanks was about 20K
USD each.  But we really don't have to worry about that anymore.  About 3
years ago, they renovated the computer room, and turned off the halon
system during the construction.  They've never bothered to turn the halon
system back on.  They probably want to save money.  So now there are about
6 fire extinguishers in the computer room.  It's the responsibility of the
computer room workers to put any fires out themselves with the fire
extinguishers.

Also, in case you don't know, New Orleans has one of the highest levels of
average humidity in the US.  There are dehumidifiers in the AC systems in
the computer room.  They have problems with the drain under the floor in
the computer room.  Recently they discovered it had back up, but they
didn't discover it until there was a few inches of water under the floor
in the computer room.  A friend of mine had to vacuum up most of it with a
wet/dry vac.  Does that VAX vacuum company in England make wet/dry vacs?

With the exception of the cost of recharging the halon system, I don't
think we've had any other disaster related costs.  Certainly no lost
systems or equipment in the 12 years I've been there.  Anyone want to
place a bet on how long before we have a disaster?

