<kerstin.hoef-emden@uni-koeln.de> wrote in message
news:8me65j$gt9$1@news.rrz.Uni-Koeln.DE...
> Hi,
>
> I have got a general question about hardware and Alpha processors. I once
> read a thread (about 2 years ago), that IDE harddisks would slow down
> Alphas. When I bought my Alpha, I was told, that I should not use
harddisks
> with speeds lower than 7200 rpms (LX164, it is a plain SCSI system). Since
> in the last c�t article about harddisks it is mentioned, that IDE speeds
are
> now enhancing to 7200 rpms, I wonder whether this might be the reason that
> IDE was considered problematic for Alphas that time? Or were there other
> reasons for not choosing them?

IDE drives can do pretty well in environments where most disk write
operations are small - 64 KB or less in Intel environments, though that may
double for Alphas with 8 KB vs. 4 KB memory pages, since the limit is
imposed by typical host controllers that can handle only about 16 memory
segments in a single request and drivers that won't allow multiple requests
to be submitted concurrently (though the newer drives reportedly support
this).  Large write transfers thus get broken up into 64 KB (128 KB?)
pieces, between each of which the disk misses a full revolution (assuming
the target sectors are contiguous).

IDE drives perform well on large (as well as small) read requests as long as
the disk read-caching feature is enabled (allowing the disk to read ahead
and avoid the missed revolutions mentioned above).  While enabling
write-caching can help write performance in a similar manner, it can also
result cause data corruption on the disk should the power fail before all
cached data has been transferred, if the host file system uses writing
algorithms that depend on writes actually being on the disk when the disk
says they've completed (VMS has such a host file system; I don't know about
Tru64 or Linux, though I think ext2fs does when used synchronously and I
strongly suspect AdvFS does).

They also suffer compared with SCSI drives in environments sufficiently
over-loaded that a bunch of (smallish) requests get queued up waiting for
the disk to serve them, since with SCSI the requests can all be submitted to
the drive and the drive has the opportunity to re-order them into the
most-efficient processing sequence (which can make a difference of a factor
of 2 or more in throughput under such loads).

In sum, the rotational speed is not the only reason IDE drives may be slower
than SCSI drives.  But under some loads, their performance may be about the
same for identical rotational speeds.

