In article <398B956B.8ACD96F1@comp2go.net>, Lisa Fortenberry <glftnbry@comp2go.net> writes:
>What would be recommended for upgrading the Proccessor on a Celebris GL
>5120?

You should probably contact the people who make the plug-in upgrades
and see what they support, since this unit is out of production.
However, from some discussions around here and what I've seen,
I can SUGGEST the following:

It has a Socket 7 CPU socket and a plug-in voltage regulator
card (your model probably just has a jumper in the voltage
regulator socket) and jumpers for the bus speed and clock
multiplier.

A 133 MHz version of this system was sold, so a 133 MHz Pentium
should plug right in with just a clock jumper change.

There was also a 166 MHz version sold: I don't know if this
model of Pentium uses the same voltage as the 120 and 133 MHz
versions.  If it does, it should just plug in with a jumper
change.  If it requires 2.8 or 2.3 Volts then you also have
to change the voltage regulator.  I'm not sure if this
part is available, which is why I suggest talking to the
people who sell CPU upgrades.  They could also tell you
if there are any plug-in upgrades with on-board clock
multipliers and/or voltage regulators.

On a purely experimental basis, I have seen one of these
systems running with a 200 MHz Pentium with MMX processor
and the plug-in voltage regulator.  The BIOS reports it
as a 166 MHz processor, but it seems to work.

If you no longer have the manuals for this system you might
want to look at 

http://www.compaq.com/support/techpubs/

where many of the technical manuals can still be retrieved.

